<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Morocco</h1><p class="st-meta">1957 CE – present · capital Rabat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent Moroccan monarchy, in which the Alaouite kings have kept central power through decades of political change and the Western Sahara dispute.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-protectorate-in-morocco">Spanish protectorate in Morocco</a> ended in 1956, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Sultan Mohammed</a> V of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a> adopted the title of king in 1957. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a> in 1958 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ifni">Ifni</a> in 1969 were reintegrated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ii-of-morocco">Hassan II</a>, king from 1961 to 1999, introduced constitutions but kept firm control, surviving coup attempts in 1971 and 1972 and repressing opposition during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/years-of-lead-morocco">Years of Lead</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a> of 1975 began Moroccan control of most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-sahara">Spanish Sahara</a>, leading to war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>. The economy rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">phosphates</a>, agriculture, tourism and remittances.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-vi-of-morocco">Mohammed VI</a> succeeded in 1999, reforming family law in 2004 and approving a new constitution after the 2011 protests, though the king retains wide powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> remains a monarchy, claiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-morocco.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Morocco" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83, Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Morocco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1957 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rabat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mohammed V, Hassan II, Mohammed VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Morocco</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=kingdom-of-morocco" aria-label="Kingdom of Morocco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4506 945 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Morocco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4826 1201L4858 1205M4826 1201L4854 1142M4826 1201L4735 1360"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4858 1205h0M4854 1142h0M4735 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4826 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4826 1201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rabat. <a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=kingdom-of-morocco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
